/-
# Counting a million matchings and getting zero

You're testing a scheduler. Forty jobs need forty workers, one job per worker.
You make a fixture you can count by hand: each pair of jobs gets its own pair
of workers, and either worker can do either job. There are 1,048,576 valid
assignments. The new counter usually returns 0.

Download this article and run `haze permanent_signs.hz` to get all the results.
On the page, each block runs with the definitions above it, including your edits.
-/

/-
## Start with two jobs

Call the jobs `build` and `lint` and the workers A and B. Give `build` to A
and `lint` has to go to B. Give `build` to B and there's one other schedule.
Twenty separate pairs give two choices each, so the test oracle is `2^20`.
Finding one schedule isn't the problem here. We're counting all of them.

![Four jobs and four workers in two groups. Each group can be matched straight across or crossed.](https://haze-lang.pages.dev/blog/images/permanent-pairs.svg)
Two pairs of workers give four schedules. A zero in the grid rules out a job-worker pairing.

Read the four arguments below as a two-row grid: `a, b` on top and `c, d`
below. A one means the worker can do the job. The product `a*d` is one if the
straight assignment works; `b*c` checks the crossed assignment. Their sum
counts the schedules. That sum is called the *permanent*. The determinant
differs by one character.

<!-- run -->

In the first query, change the third argument to `0`: `lint` can no longer
run on A. The count drops from two to one. `#eval` asks Haze what an expression
returns. You can run any block without first clicking the ones above it.
-/

def perm2(a : Num, b : Num, c : Num, d : Num) : Num := a*d + b*c
def det2(a : Num, b : Num, c : Num, d : Num) : Num := a*d - b*c

#eval perm2(1, 1, 1, 1)
#eval det2(1, 1, 1, 1)

/-
## Trace one call to the counter

There are fast algorithms for determinants. Godsil and Gutman's estimator
uses one as a counting shortcut: leave every zero alone, replace each one
with an independent random sign, take the determinant, then square it.

```signs
+1  +1
+1  +1
```

Click the bottom-right sign. The determinant changes from zero to minus two;
its square changes from zero to four. Now try *all* sixteen sign assignments
in the code below. `flip(1/2)` is a fair coin. Haze enumerates the outcomes
with their probabilities, so running this block twice gives the same table.
`#expect` asks for the weighted mean.

<!-- run -->

Eight assignments return zero. Eight return four. A single call never gives
the right count, two, but the mean is right. That part isn't luck. Expand the
squared determinant of any zero-one matrix. For two different matchings there
is an edge used by only one of them. Flipping its sign cancels their cross term
in the average. A matching paired with itself contributes one.
-/

def sign() : Num := if flip(1/2) then 1 else -1
def square(x : Num) : Num := x*x
def fresh() : Num := square(det2(sign(), sign(), sign(), sign()))

#eval fresh()
#expect fresh()

/-
## A broken version worth keeping in the test

Suppose a refactor saves one coin flip. It draws `a`, `b`, and `c` and sets
the fourth sign to `a*b*c`. Each sign is still individually fair. Even a test
that inspects any *three* signs passes: given any three, the missing sign is
uniquely determined, and each triple has probability `1/8`.

The determinant notices. Substituting for `d` gives `a*d = a*a*b*c = b*c`,
so the subtraction always produces zero. Keep both versions of the generator
so we can compare them on the same fixture.

<!-- run -->

Try replacing `a*b*c` in `tied` with `sign()`. The theorem should now fail:
we gave the fourth sign its independence back. Use **Reset edits** on this
block to put the bug back for the comparison below.
-/

enum Wiring = Independent | Parity

def tied(a : Num, b : Num, c : Num) : Num := square(det2(a, b, c, a*b*c))
def block(w : Wiring) : Num :=
  if w == Independent then fresh() else tied(sign(), sign(), sign())

theorem parity_always_cancels : block(Parity) == 0

/-
## Run the forty-job fixture

Each pair of jobs occupies a separate block of the matrix. The determinant
of the whole matrix is the product of those block determinants, so we can
multiply the squared results as we go. Once the product reaches zero the
remaining blocks can't change it. One step below processes one pair of jobs.

<!-- run -->

Try `after[2]` in the first query: the counter returns 0 three quarters of the
time and 16 the other quarter. For the original twenty pairs, the only nonzero
answer is `4^20`, or 1,099,511,627,776. It takes twenty independent nonzero
blocks to get it, so it happens once in 1,048,576 draws on average. Multiply
the payoff by that probability and you get exactly 1,048,576 schedules.

This sparse matrix is an old objection to the estimator; Costello and Vu
[credit Barvinok for it](https://arxiv.org/abs/0905.1909) in section 2. A
small test that only compares the expected value to the oracle misses it.
-/

system Blocks(w : Wiring) {
  var estimate : Num := 1
  step { if estimate != 0 { estimate := estimate * block(w) } }
}

#eval Blocks(Independent) |= after[20] estimate
#expect Blocks(Independent) |= after[20] estimate
#prob Blocks(Independent) |= after[20] estimate != 0

/-
## Give it a million tries

Run the published twenty-block estimator 1,048,576 independent times and
average the answers. One nonzero draw makes the average exactly right. Two
make it double. If every draw is zero, the average is still zero.

The chance of that last outcome is `(1 - 1/1048576)^1048576`. You can check
it without sampling or printing a fraction with millions of digits. Start
with the chance of one miss. Square it to get the chance of two independent
misses, then four, then eight. Twenty squarings cover all 1,048,576 draws.
The code keeps ten decimal places and rounds down and up separately, so the
true percentage stays between the two outputs.

<!-- run -->

Both ends round to **36.8%**. Change both step counts to `21` to double the
number of trials on the same fixture. The chance of a page full of zeros is
still about 13.5%. These are odds *given that the generator is correct*.
Seeing zeros is not enough to diagnose the three-bit bug.
-/

def down(x : Num) : Num := floor(x * 10000000000) / 10000000000

system Misses {
  var lo : Num := 1 - 1/1048576
  var hi : Num := 1 - 1/1048576
  step {
    lo := down(lo * lo)
    hi := -down(-(hi * hi))
  }
}

#eval Misses |= after[20] 100 * lo
#eval Misses |= after[20] 100 * hi

/-
## Which generator did we ship?

Imagine you have a binary and know it contains one of the two generators.
You don't know which. `param` below lists both possibilities and keeps the
choice fixed across all twenty blocks. A weight of `1` means “don't rule
this one out.” Two ones aren't two probabilities of one half.

<!-- run -->

The mean could be zero or 1,048,576. The last two lines ask a different
question: what if the program itself tossed a fair coin to pick a generator
at startup? Then the mean is 524,288. You don't get that number just by
being unsure which binary is installed.

For a regression test, look at the product of the four signs in one block.
With independent signs it's negative half the time. With the three-bit
shortcut it's always positive. That's a much cheaper test than waiting for
a nonzero answer on the forty-job fixture.
-/

param wiring : Wiring ~= {Independent: 1, Parity: 1}
#expect Blocks(wiring) |= after[20] estimate
#prob Blocks(wiring) |= after[20] estimate != 0

input lottery : Wiring ~ {Independent: 1/2, Parity: 1/2}
#expect Blocks(lottery) |= after[20] estimate

/-
## Where VP and VNP come in

The [HN discussion](https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50620102) and
[X post](https://x.com/Liam06972452/status/2124990562906304556) were about
an [Anthropic preprint](https://github.com/anthropics/formal-math/tree/main/vp-vnp);
a [separate OpenAI preprint](https://github.com/openai/math/tree/main/preprints/VP-and-VNP-are-different-November-23-2026)
also claims VP ≠ VNP. That's an arithmetic-circuit question, not Boolean
P versus NP. The distinction matters for this shortcut.

A determinant has polynomial-size arithmetic circuits. For a matrix with
`m` ones the averaging identity can have `2^m` sign assignments. A small
circuit for one draw doesn't give you a small circuit that computes their
mean *exactly*. Try asking how often one draw even equals the mean in our
fixture:

<!-- run -->

For the formal connection: VP uses polynomial-size arithmetic circuits for
families with polynomially bounded degree and number of variables. VNP also
allows a sum over polynomially many extra Boolean variables. The permanent
is VNP-complete outside characteristic two; in characteristic two the signs
coincide and the permanent equals the determinant. The preprints use the
Hamiltonian cycle polynomial for the all-fields statement. [Lance Fortnow](https://blog.computationalcomplexity.org/2026/11/vp-vnp.html)
explains the distinction. This experiment doesn't verify either proof.

Nor does this fixture rule out good approximations: [Jerrum, Sinclair and
Vigoda](https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~sinclair/perm2.pdf) gave a
randomized polynomial-time approximation scheme for nonnegative matrices by
a different method. For the scheduler test, keep the exact two-to-a-pair
oracle. It gives you something a million runs of the shortcut might not:
one answer you can put in an assertion.
-/

#prob Blocks(Independent) |= after[20] estimate == 1048576
